forensic experts will try to identify  the presumed remains of dozens of  Americans killed in the Korean War  fifty-five flag covered caskets were met  by Vice President pence and military  honor guards yesterday when they arrived  at a military base in Hawaii North  Korea's leader promised to their return  at his June summit with President Trump  the President thanked North Korean  leader Kim jong-un on Twitter for  sending the remains  vladimir duthiers of our streaming  network cbsn shows us what their return  means for the families after more than  60 years service members presumed killed  in the Korean War arrived on US soil  Wednesday their flag draped casket  slowly filling a military hangar in  Honolulu today begins a new season of  hope for the families of our missing  fallen hope that after so many years of  questions these families will have  closure  many of these photos we actually got  from the families back in March we spoke  with the government agency that accounts  for Americans lost in conflict in this  room and along this table you can see  some of the artifacts that the PIO wmia  accounting agency laboratory have  collected items that soldiers sailors  Marines Airmen would have carried with  them into battle through lab analysis of  artifacts bones and DNA the agency works  to identify the soldiers and bring their  families closure when these guys went  missing it like created a big hole in  their life what we're trying to do is  fill that hole as much as we can but the  lab now faces a challenge there was only  one dog tag amid the 55 sets of remains  and when North Korea has returned  remains in decades past the bones of  different people have reportedly been  combined together or mixed with animal  remains initial reports say these new  remains appear to be in a similar state  of disarray meaning the identifying  process could take years  still many believe getting these  servicemembers back on US soil brings  them one step closer to their loved ones  some have called the Korean War the  Forgotten War but today we prove these  heroes were never forgotten for CBS this  morning  vladimir duthiers los angeles ving story
 

